{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=0 \\\\ -6x+8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+4y=0\\,⇒\\,4y=3x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{3}{4}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "064d49688089ffc8e3846eddc156ae64993666125545844f41bf2c81fe699a88"} {"question" "q-333801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{4}{3}x"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3}{4}x"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3}{4}x-1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{4}{3}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{3}{4}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "064d49688089ffc8e3846eddc156ae64993666125545844f41bf2c81fe699a88"} {"question" "q-333824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/06b9fd74afcc99a3396aa280b248272904c495d6181e6a5c027c0cd6a30acbfb.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/a695d58031c47e43d0aed186e271eff7d3da271ea83ae3c053be011afe9d4707.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "064d49688089ffc8e3846eddc156ae64993666125545844f41bf2c81fe699a88"} {"question" "q-333832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/59b96e2396db2559e9893c73ef530a312a347442b9c4fde0137583310ceba0a9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/8a7b634d05a938d9b481620da9837a8f28dc28f84ac18a3f9a20678ead0df20b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/dd3667299cd5da8fe5d9450799fbe865e832449ac39427795a5b87cf2786f587.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f4b88938a280acb1149be29d6832483713437236424bbb640784a55bafea712c.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-333832" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/9988933a3f4393f745b1df6f0dc667a4074f5ee787fab43fb392b117368a8ca9.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/2809f28b49c9782db6fdf3f5a8be7fd99ec6d9a08db8e81d91c4d6bc53efcdda.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7fd67e8a768419dd497bc6345c5999ff921e6de492f60c5f217e93f8dc4d07c1.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "064d49688089ffc8e3846eddc156ae64993666125545844f41bf2c81fe699a88"} {"question" "q-334385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/568be19a1553bf837519d82e11f8ee391c7dce120605fc2855039eeff83ff1fd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7705e0976e796f3a5642d68287dcfef5315c6599f7e997b56a34ec534b89e76d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=9t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/state.json" "file_sha256" "b31f040eab47324889c134ab7b925915cd7f4aa12671e31ebbea6ca7b35b8b97"} {"question" "q-334638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "064d49688089ffc8e3846eddc156ae64993666125545844f41bf2c81fe699a88"} {"question" "q-334638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/